# REL-10: One bad relation stops all relations (Roam)

- Worker: `repro-REL-10`
- Candidate SHA: `b3796a70b526541014c92a40f685389060b862fe`
- Worktree / branch: `/mnt/data/projects/discourse-graph/.claude/worktrees/push-pull-repros` / `push-pull-repros`
- Test in worktree: `apps/roam/src/utils/__tests__/repro-REL-10.test.ts` (untracked, not committed)
- Copy: `/mnt/data/projects/dg-validation-eng-1883/matrix/repros/REL-10/repro-REL-10.test.ts`
- Command (from `apps/roam`): `npx vitest run --config vitest.config.mts src/utils/__tests__/repro-REL-10.test.ts`
- Raw output: `vitest-output.txt` (exact command). `vitest-output-verbose.txt` is the same run with `--reporter=verbose` added. Vitest 4's default reporter only prints console output for failing tests, and the verbose run also shows the `ACTUAL:` lines of the passing controls.
- Vitest: `vitest/4.1.6 linux-x64 node-v20.20.2`
- Directions covered: OR (Obsidian source `obsidian:remote-vault`, triples point at a relation type schema) and RR (Roam source `https://roamresearch.com/#/app/remote-graph`, triples carry label and complement inline). The destination is a Roam graph named `local` in both cases.

This test supports its own assertion only. It is not a completed UI case: no dialog was rendered and no Roam or Obsidian app was run.

## Result: reproduced

4 failed, 4 passed (8 tests). The 4 failures are the reproduction and the 4 passes are controls.

## Expected (case row)

The other relations still import.

## Fixture

- Local node types: `local-claim` (Claim) and `local-evidence` (Evidence). Incoming node schemas match them by label.
- Local relation schemas: `local-supports-a` "Supports" and `local-supports-b` "supports", both Claim to Claim with distinct ids. Also `local-informs` "Informs", Evidence to Claim.
- Incoming: the triple `remote-supports-triple` (Supports, Claim to Claim) matches both Supports schemas. The triple `remote-informs-triple` (Informs, Evidence to Claim) matches only `local-informs`. Each triple has one relation instance, and every endpoint resolves to an imported page through the mocked `findImportedNodeUidBySourceRid`.
- Mocked: discovery, `getDiscourseRelations`/`getDiscourseNodes` (settings), the source-identity reads and writes, `createRelationSchema`, `createReifiedRelation`/`getReifiedRelations`, `deleteBlock`, `refreshConfigTree`, and `window.roamAlphaAPI`. The real `importSharedRelations`, `matchImportedNodeSchemas`, `matchImportedRelationSchemas`, `importRelations`, `findTargetUid` and RID helpers run unchanged.

## Actual on the candidate

The same result in OR and RR:

| Case | Order (triples and instances) | Outcome | Reified relations created |
|---|---|---|---|
| Two schemas, same label | ambiguous, then independent | rejects `"multiple matches"` | none |
| Two schemas, same label | independent, then ambiguous | rejects `"multiple matches"` | none |
| Control: one schema id, two query patterns | ambiguous, then independent | resolves | both (`local-supports-a`, `local-informs`) |
| Control: independent triple only (same two local schemas) | independent | resolves | `local-informs` only |

In both failing cases, the test logs 0 endpoint lookups, 0 `createRelationSchema` calls and 0 `refreshConfigTree` calls. The instance loop never starts.

**Order does not matter.** The independent relation is lost even when its triple schema and its instance come first. `matchImportedRelationSchemas` maps the independent triple first and then throws on the ambiguous triple. The throw discards the whole mapping, so the instance loop never receives it.

The dialog text, built from the template at `DiscoverSharedNodesDialog.tsx:346` with the real `getErrorMessage`, would read: `The nodes were imported, but their relations were not: multiple matches`.

## Hypothesis in the row's "why", checked against the code

- **Confirmed: the "multiple matches" throw ends the whole relation step.** At `importSharedRelations.ts:136-146`, triples are matched on case-insensitive label plus source and destination, matching ids are deduplicated, and `throw new Error("multiple matches")` runs when more than one distinct id matches. Nothing catches the error inside `matchImportedRelationSchemas` (`:81-178`) or inside `importSharedRelations` (`:243-260`). Schema matching (`:251`) has to finish before `importRelations` runs (`:257`), so a throw at this stage stops every instance, including unrelated ones (the instance loop is at `:186-240`). `refreshConfigTree` (`:259`) is skipped too.
- **Confirmed: the dialog wording.** At `DiscoverSharedNodesDialog.tsx:339-348`, the dialog calls `importSharedRelations` after `importSharedNodes` (`:307`) has already written the nodes. It catches the rejection and passes `userMessage: \`The nodes were imported, but their relations were not: ${getErrorMessage(relationsError)}\``. `getErrorMessage` (`getErrorMessage.ts:6-13`) returns `Error.message`, so the user sees `...were not: multiple matches`. The message names no relation, type or schema.
- **Confirmed: one schema with duplicated query patterns is not the trigger.** `:142-143` deduplicates by id. The control passes in both directions, and both relations import against `local-supports-a`.
- **Beyond the row, from code reading only (not tested):** `discoverSharedRelations.ts:77-100` returns every shared relation whose endpoints are local or already imported, plus the endpoints in `futureImportRids`, not only relations of the nodes just selected. So once a destination graph has two schemas with the same label and endpoint types, any import that discovers one relation of the matching type blocks relation import for that run. Also, triples processed before the throw can already have created local relation schemas and written their provenance (`:150-162`), and those writes are not rolled back.

## Limits

- Code-level only. The UI toast or dialog was not rendered. Real Roam settings parsing (`getDiscourseRelations`), real discovery from Supabase and real block writes are mocked.
- The fixture's independent triple matches an existing local schema. The case where a new schema is created before the throw is described from code only.
- The two orders flip triple order and instance order together. A separate instance-only order test is unnecessary because the instance loop is never reached, which the zero endpoint lookups show. Per-instance throws inside `importRelations` (`:192-199`, `:226`) do depend on order, but they are a different trigger and are not covered here.
- In the fixture, `relationType` on a relation instance is the triple schema's local id. This matches the real pipeline: `discoverSharedRelations` rewrites each Obsidian instance's `schema_id` to its triple before `dbRelationToCrossApp` converts it. The rewrite itself is not exercised, because discovery is mocked.

